<node id="1218685" title="Re^2: Syntax Perl Version support $c = () = $a =~ /\./g" created="2018-07-17 15:13:16" updated="2018-07-17 15:13:16" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;AFAIK, Perl 5.x has always supported this&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I'm having some trouble running a bisect at the moment, but as a tentative result, it appears that Perl versions roughly before 5.004 didn't like the test program &lt;c&gt;-e '$a="f.o.o.bar"; $b=()=$a=~/\./g; $b==3 or die $b'&lt;/c&gt; because &lt;c&gt;Can't modify stub in list assignment at -e line 1, near "/\./g;"&lt;/c&gt;. AFAICT it was not possible to assign to an empty list back then (a workaround seems to be &lt;c&gt;$b = @{[]} = $a =~ /\./g;&lt;/c&gt;, which works on 5.000).&lt;/p&gt;
</doctext>
